{
  "abstract": "This work presents new constraints on the existence and the binding energy of a possible $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ bound state, the H-dibaryon, derived from $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ femtoscopic measurements by the ALICE collaboration. The results are obtained from a new measurement using the femtoscopy technique in pp collisions at $\\sqrt{s}=13$ TeV and p-Pb collisions at $\\sqrt{s_{\\mathrm{NN}}}=5.02$ TeV, combined with previously published results from p-Pb collisions at $\\sqrt{s}=7$ TeV. The $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ scattering parameter space, spanned by the inverse scattering length $f_0^{-1}$ and the effective range $d_0$, is constrained by comparing the measured $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ correlation function with calculations obtained within the Lednicky model. The data are compatible with hypernuclei results and lattice computations, both predicting a shallow attractive interaction, and permit to test different theoretical approaches describing the $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ interaction. The region in the $(f_0^{-1},d_0)$ plane which would accommodate a $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ bound state is substantially restricted compared to previous studies. The binding energy of the possible $\\Lambda$-$\\Lambda$ bound state is estimated within an effective-range expansion approach and is found to be $B_{\\Lambda\\Lambda}=3.2^{+1.6}_{-2.4}\\mathrm{(stat)}^{+1.8}_{-1.0}\\mathrm{(syst)}$ MeV.",
